  <text>
      <body>
         <entry xml:id="burgar-dlgenr"
            xml:lang="en"
            type="mainEntry"
            prev="#"
            next="#"
            source="Genesis Rabbah">
            <form type="lemma" opt="false">
               <orth type="lemma" xml:lang="jpa" opt="false" select="true">בורגר</orth>
               <orth type="transliteration" xml:lang="fjb" opt="false">bwrgwr</orth>
               <pron notation="rabbinic">burgar</pron>
               <gramGrp>
                  <gram type="pos">noun</gram>
                  <gram type="gen">m.</gram>
               </gramGrp>
            </form>
            <sense xml:id="burgar-dlgenr-1">
               <cit type="translation" xml:lang="jpa" subtype="sense">
                  <quote>land-worker, farmer, husbandman</quote>
               </cit>
               <cit type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="grc">
                  <quote>βουργάριος</quote>
               </cit>
               <cit type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="la">
                  <quote>burgarius</quote>
               </cit>
               <cit type="example" xml:lang="jpa">
                  <quote>איש האדמה 
                     <app>
                        <lem>בורגר</lem>
                        <rdg wit="#Ox1">ברגר</rdg>
                        <rdg wit="#K V">לשם בורגר</rdg>
                     </app> לשם 
                     <app>
                        <lem>בורגרותה</lem>
                        <rdg wit="#K">בורגרות</rdg>
                        <rdg wit="#V Ox2">בורגרות</rdg>
                        </app></quote>
                  <bibl>
                     <ref type="witness">36,3</ref>
                     <ref type="witness">I 337: 8</ref>
                  </bibl>
                  <cit type="translation" xml:lang="en">
                     <quote>[And Noah] the husbandman [lit. ‘A man of the ground’] (Gen 9,20): [He was] a land-worker in order to deal with husbandry.</quote>
                  </cit>
                  <note>= <bibl type="ancient">Yalq 61 [16c: 50]</bibl></note>
               </cit>
            </sense>
            <note>For this etymology see also MY ad loc.; <bibl type="modern">Fürst 80a</bibl>. <bibl type="modern">Levy I 203a</bibl>, <bibl type="modern">Krauss, LW II 144a</bibl> explain as Gr. <term xml:lang="grc">γεωργός</term>. Löw in Krauss ad loc. doubts the etymology „burgarius“, since this word is of “a very late origin”. <bibl type="modern">Sperber (2012) 185</bibl>, however, supports this etymology. <bibl type="modern">Sokoloff, DJPA 69b-10a</bibl> connects with Gr. <term xml:lang="grc">πύργος</term> (acc. to <bibl type="modern">Sperber, Essays on Greek and Latin in the Mishna, Talmud and Midrashic Literature, Jerusalem 1982, 116ff.</bibl>). <term xml:lang="jpa">בורגרותה</term> <term xml:lang="fjb">bwrgrwth</term> <term xml:lang="rabbinic">burgeruta</term> (<bibl type="modern">Levy I 203a</bibl>) is a hebr. neologism meaning “husbandary”. Instead of <term xml:lang="jpa">לשם</term> shall be prob. be read <term xml:lang="jpa">לשון</term> (like in ...) “this means”.</note>
            <ref type="bibliography">
               <bibl>
                  <title>AC II</title>
                  <citedRange>185ab</citedRange></bibl>
            </ref>
         </entry>
